<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jakhanke</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jahanka, Diakhanke, Diakhanké</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jakhanke are a Manding-speaking people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a> region, often classed as a subgroup of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soninke">Soninke</a>, who historically formed an endogamous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> of Muslim clerics and teachers. Centred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, with smaller communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, they have been part of the region since the 15th century and helped spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manding">Manding</a> peoples. Their tradition stressed learning, the teaching of the young, the rejection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> and keeping out of politics; when threatened, they moved their villages. From at least the 17th century they also traded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, salt, cloth, gold and slaves, and their farms relied on slave labour.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some clerical towns, such as Dia, were left to govern themselves by Islamic law alone under the <b>Jakhanke</b>, followers of the quietist teaching of al-Hajj Salim Suwari.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mali-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</a></li></ul></section>
